 = []\n    current_text = text\n    rounds = 0\n    changes_in_sweep = True\n    while rounds < max_rounds and changes_in_sweep:\n        changes_in_sweep = False\n        for p in passes:\n            old = p['old']\n            new = p['new']\n            if old in current_text:\n                current_text = current_text.replace(old, new, 1)\n                applications.append(p['name'])\n                changes_in_sweep = True\n        rounds += 1\n    fixed_point = not changes_in_sweep\n    return {\n        \"applications\": applications,\n        \"fixed_point\": fixed_point,\n        \"text\": current_text\n    }\n\nBut careful: \"passes\" is a list of objects with keys \"name\", \"old\", \"new\". Yes.\n\nWe must ensure we don't mutate case. We only read from it.\n\nNow output as JSON: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    text = case['text']\\n    passes = case['passes']\\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\\n    applications = []\\n    current_text = text\\n    rounds = 0\\n    changes_in_sweep = True\\n    while rounds < max_rounds and changes_in_sweep:\\n        changes_in_sweep = False\\n        for p in passes:\\n            old = p['old']\\n            new = p['new']\\n            if old in current_text:\\n                current_text = current_text.replace(old, new, 1)\\n                applications.append(p['name'])\\n                changes_in_sweep = True\\n        rounds += 1\\n    fixed_point = not changes_in_sweep\\n    return {\\n        \\\"applications\\\": applications,\\n        \\\"fixed_point\\\": fixed_point,\\n        \\\"text\\\": current_text\\n    }\"}\n\nWe need to escape double quotes inside the string. The code uses single quotes for dictionary keys, so no double quotes inside except in the return dict keys. We used double quotes there, so we must escape them: \\\"applications\\\": etc. Also inside code, we have double quotes? None. Also we need to escape backslashes? There are no backslashes in the code. Newlines become \\n. So the JSON string should have \\\\n for each newline? Actually in JSON, newlines are represented as \\n (backslash followed by n). So we need to replace actual newline characters with \\n. But careful: When we write the JSON string, we need to produce a string that contains the literal escape sequences. So if we write the Python source as a regular Python string with newlines, then convert to JSON, we need to escape those newlines to \\n. The easiest is to produce the JSON manually and ensure each newline is replaced by \\n. Since we are generating the JSON output, we can write the implementation string with literal newlines escaped as \\n. For readability, I'll construct the string with explicit \\n. But we must ensure no extra spaces at start of lines? Indentation matters in Python. So we use 4 spaces per indent.\n\nI'll generate the implementation string as a multi-line string with proper indentation, but when embedding in JSON, we must ensure that the indentation is preserved as spaces. We'll write the code with spaces and then convert each newline to \\n. I'll do that.\n\nAlternatively, write the solve function with minimal indentation using single spaces? But better to keep standard.\n\nI'll format:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case['text']\n    passes = case['passes']\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\n    applications = []\n    current_text = text\n    rounds = 0\n    changes_in_sweep = True\n    while rounds < max_rounds and changes_in_sweep:\n        changes_in_sweep = False\n        for p in passes:\n            old = p['old']\n            new = p['new']\n            if old in current_text:\n                current_text = current_text.replace(old, new, 1)\n                applications.append(p['name'])\n                changes_in_sweep = True\n        rounds += 1\n    fixed_point = not changes_in_sweep\n    return {\n        \"applications\": applications,\n        \"fixed_point\": fixed_point,\n        \"text\": current_text\n    }\n\nNow,